Z||000000||RobertVol||04-09-2001||12:05 AM||||I have seen many topics on Maaco and other paint shops. Most of the time its about how crappy the paint job is but in most cases its the lower levels that were purchased. If you go with the top of the line Maaco service which I think runs like 599 or 699.00 is it any good? Of course you get what you pay for but you know how it is..... <P>Budgets and all that  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Any opinions? Anyone got the higher levels jobs and liked it ? <P>Thanks<P>------------------<BR>Robert<BR>88GT 56k T TOP  <BR>North Florida Fiero Club<P>CLICK BELOW AND SEE OUR SITE !!! <A HREF="http://fullspeed.to/NorthFLFieros" TARGET=_blank>http://fullspeed.to/NorthFLFieros</A><BR>   <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=819451&a=5959473&p=36934984&Sequence=1">  <p>[This message has been edited by RobertVol (edited 04-09-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||Wade87GT||04-09-2001||12:30 AM||||About 4 years ago my Black 87 GT needed a repaint really bad. The clearcoat had almost completely hazed over. Most of the shops around here were asking over $2,000 for a regular paint job, which included runs and a lot of over-spray (from the examples I saw). I took it to Fact-O-Bake (like Maaco) in Pensacola and they did a better job for $460. So I guess it depends on who's shooting the paint, not where or for how much.<P>Wade||||reg||1
Z||000002||RobertVol||04-09-2001||12:38 AM||||True True.<P>Good point Wade ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||04-09-2001||12:47 AM||||Macco's are very "iffy" The one near my house has painted a few cars (even the higher quality/expensive paint) and still managed to screw it up. But there is one about 15 miles north of me that has done very excellent work even on the "discount"<BR>jobs. I would suggest you check out all the Macco's or other body shops. It seems not so much of the company name but who does the work. Also you can check with local car clubs for recommendations. Corvette clubs are extremely picky when it come to paint jobs. A friend had a 70 olds 442 he wanted to have flames done on it. He called numerous body shops and he was quoted $2000 and up. He went to a local car club that deel with 30's and 40's hot rods. They sent him to a small shop way out in the boonies and they painted the flames on for $150.00<BR>They came out perfect. My advise is to ask around. Most areas have numerous clubs, see who recommends who.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Taijiguy||04-09-2001||12:12 PM||||The biggest problem with Maaco for example, is they don't do the kind of prep work it takes to doa quality job. If you do some of your own prep work, you might even get a better deal. I know they generally paint right over old pinstriping, even if it's tape. They just paint over it, and apply new pinstriping. It's a mess. I agree with Oreif, find a small mom and pop place, usually some old guy who's been painting for a hundred years, and knows his stuff, and does it more for the enjoyment than the money.||||reg||1
